## Source 192

Part a asks for ordered two-digit numbers divisible by 13 and the terms at
positions 1, 4, 7. Put each number equal to 13m, with m a positive integer.
The condition 10 <= 13m <= 99 gives 1 <= m <= 7. Thus the full finite sequence
is 13, 26, 39, 52, 65, 78, 91, with b_1 = 13, b_4 = 52, b_7 = 91.
The multiplier 1 is valid because the TERM 13, not the multiplier, must have
two digits. Excluding only 13 changes the length and all positional assignments.
The response must retain both eligibility and ordering, rather than judge an
isolated multiplication.

Part b asks for the rule applied to six indices. The odd indices 1 and 253
give -1; the even indices 4 and 100 give 0. For every admissible natural k,
2k is even and 2k+1 is odd, so z_(2k) = 0 and z_(2k+1) = -1.
The seed defines sequence positions from 1; symbolic claims are for indices
that exist in that domain, without independently resolving whether N includes
zero. A useful integrated judgment groups indices with equal term values
and compares those groups: z_1 = z_253 = z_(2k+1) < z_4 = z_100 = z_(2k).
It must require applying parity to all four concrete and both symbolic indices,
not merely copy the given general rule or expose six disconnected answer slots.
Choice recognition still does not establish independently writing all six terms.

## Source 193

There are exactly six positions, ordered a_1 through a_6.
The immediate successors of a_1 and a_4 are a_2 and a_5, respectively.
The immediate predecessors of a_2 and a_5 are a_1 and a_4, respectively.
Only a_1 has no predecessor; only a_6 has no successor.
Equal numerical term values, if any, would not change adjacency of positions.
A complete report must determine forward neighbors, backward neighbors and
both finite endpoints. To make predecessor judgment necessary, there must be
a competing complete report matching the valid forward and endpoint statements
but differing on backward neighbors. Recognizing that report does not prove
independent construction of the full written neighbor description.

## Source 199

Part a: long division gives 7/12 = 0.583333..., with 3 recurring after 58.
Rounding the entire infinite decimal afresh to n decimal places gives
x_1 = 0.6, x_2 = 0.58, x_3 = 0.583,
x_10 = 0.5833333333. At n = 1 the next digit is 8, so 5 rounds up to 6.
At n >= 2 the next discarded digit is always 3, so no increment is made.
For n >= 2, x_n = 7/12 - (1/3) * 10^(-n).
Consequently x_(n+1) - x_n = 3 * 10^(-(n+1)) > 0 in that tail.
The first term is above 7/12, and every later term is below 7/12; the tail
is strictly increasing. Hence no pair of terms is equal. Since x_2 < x_1
but x_3 > x_2, the whole sequence is neither increasing nor decreasing
under the seed's strict adjacent-term definitions.
Repeated decimal DIGITS are not repeated real-number TERMS: for example,
x_10 < x_11, the latter being 0.58333333333.
All displayed rounded values, whole-sequence classification and later-term
distinctness are substantive tasks. A process report should leave a rival
that has the valid displayed values and global classification but wrongly
freezes later values after a repeating digit pattern begins; otherwise the
equality distinction can be ignored.

Part b: 23/45 = 0.511111..., with 1 recurring after 5.
Rounding to n decimal places never increments the retained prefix because
the next digit is 1. Thus y_1 = 0.5, y_2 = 0.51, and in general
y_n = 23/45 - (1/9) * 10^(-n). The decimal 0.5111111111 has ten
decimal places, so it is y_10. Every next term adds a positive digit 1
at the new place, hence the whole sequence is strictly increasing.
This sibling task is solved here for source-level completeness, but its
existing candidate unit is outside this author's edit authority.

